Based on the provided reference context, the study investigated whether the combination of 2-methoxyestradiol (2ME) with various chemotherapeutic agents could produce an additive inhibitory effect on the proliferation of human ovarian cancer cells. Specifically, it was observed that for both of the ovary cancer cell lines examined, there was a significant additive effect of 2ME with epirubicine and carboplatin at the lower concentration ranges of these chemotherapeutic substances. This suggests that the presence of 2ME allows for potentially lower doses of epirubicine and carboplatin to achieve the same degree of anticancer effect compared to using these chemotherapeutics alone. Consequently, this finding implies that the combination with 2ME might enable the use of reduced dosages of these chemotherapeutic agents in the treatment of human ovarian cancer, thereby possibly reducing side effects associated with higher chemotherapeutic dosages.

